# OAuth 2.0 Demonstrating Proof of Possession (DPoP)

**Demonstrating Proof of Possession (DPoP)** is an application-level mechanism for *sender-constraining* OAuth 2.0 access tokens (and potentially refresh tokens) using proof-of-possession of an asymmetric key. A DPoP client includes a signed **DPoP proof JWT** in a `DPoP` HTTP request header; the authorization server can bind issued tokens to the corresponding public key, and a resource server can verify that the token presenter also possesses the private key.

DPoP is standardized as **RFC 9449** (Standards Track, September 2023). [RFC 9449](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449)

## Overview

At a high level, a DPoP-enabled flow looks like:

1. The client has (or generates) an asymmetric key pair.
2. The client sends a **DPoP proof** in the `DPoP` request header alongside relevant OAuth requests.
3. The authorization server issues a token that is *bound* to the public key.
4. When calling a protected resource, the client sends:
   - the access token using the **DPoP** HTTP authentication scheme (`Authorization: DPoP <token>`), and
   - a fresh DPoP proof in the `DPoP` header.

A resource server MUST verify the DPoP proof and confirm that the proof’s public key matches the key to which the access token is bound. [RFC 9449, Section 7.1](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-7.1)

## DPoP proof JWT

A DPoP proof is a **JWT** carried in the `DPoP` HTTP request header. It is signed with a client-held private key, and the corresponding public key is conveyed in the JWT header as a **JWK**. [RFC 9449, Section 4](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-4)

### Required JOSE header parameters

The JOSE header MUST include at least: [RFC 9449, Section 4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-4.2)

- `typ`: must be `dpop+jwt`
- `alg`: an **asymmetric** JWS algorithm (must not be `none` or a symmetric MAC algorithm)
- `jwk`: the public key in JWK format (must not contain a private key)

### Required claims

The payload MUST include at least: [RFC 9449, Section 4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-4.2)

- `jti`: unique identifier for the proof; RFC 9449 notes it can be used for replay detection/prevention
- `htm`: the HTTP method of the request
- `htu`: the HTTP target URI of the request **without query and fragment parts**
- `iat`: creation timestamp

When the proof is used with protected resource access, it MUST also include:

- `ath`: base64url-encoded SHA-256 hash of the ASCII encoding of the associated access token’s value [RFC 9449, Section 4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-4.2)

When a server provides a nonce via the `DPoP-Nonce` response header, the proof MUST also include:

- `nonce`: the recent nonce value provided by the server [RFC 9449, Section 4.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-4.2)

Each HTTP request requires a **unique** DPoP proof. [RFC 9449, Section 4](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-4)

## Proof validation (what servers check)

RFC 9449 specifies a set of checks for validating DPoP proofs, including:

- the proof is a well-formed JWT and verifies against the public key in `jwk`
- `htm` matches the current request’s method
- `htu` matches the current request URI **ignoring query and fragment**
- if the server provided a nonce, `nonce` matches
- the proof’s creation time (from `iat` or nonce-based server timestamping) is within an acceptable window

Servers are advised to apply URI normalization before comparing `htu`. [RFC 9449, Section 4.3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-4.3)

## Protected resource access and the DPoP authentication scheme

When accessing a DPoP-protected resource, the request MUST include both:

- a DPoP proof (per Section 4), and
- the access token using the **DPoP** authentication scheme in the `Authorization` header.

The proof MUST include an `ath` claim with a valid hash of the associated access token. [RFC 9449, Section 7](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-7)

The DPoP authentication scheme uses `Authorization: DPoP <token68>`. [RFC 9449, Section 7.1](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-7.1)

### Bearer/DPoP downgrade considerations

If a protected resource supports both Bearer and DPoP schemes, it MUST reject a DPoP-bound access token that is received as a bearer token (to prevent downgrade). [RFC 9449, Section 7.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-7.2)

## Token binding and public-key confirmation (`cnf` / `jkt`)

Resource servers need a way to determine which public key an access token is bound to.

When access tokens are JWTs, RFC 9449 defines a confirmation method using the `cnf` claim with a `jkt` member:

- `jkt` is the base64url-encoded **JWK SHA-256 thumbprint** (per RFC 7638) of the DPoP public key to which the access token is bound. [RFC 9449, Section 6.1](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-6.1)

For token introspection, the same `cnf` / `jkt` structure is used as top-level JSON in the introspection response; if `token_type` is included, it MUST be `DPoP`. [RFC 9449, Section 6.2](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9449#section-6.2)

